Compute session packet timestamps in 64 bits.

IHS_SessionPacketTimestamp multiplied tv_nsec by 65536 before widening the result. Where long
is 32 bits, as in WebAssembly, the product overflows for most of every second, so the
sub-second part of every timestamp was garbage: in the packets sent to Steam, in ACKs, and in
the round trip measured from them (a TV showed 14464000 ms). IHS_TimerNow already widens
before multiplying; this does the same.

diff --git a/src/session/packet.c b/src/session/packet.c
index f2fd260..64984a9 100644
--- a/src/session/packet.c
+++ b/src/session/packet.c
@@ -134,7 +134,9 @@ void IHS_SessionPacketClear(IHS_SessionPacket *packet, bool freeData) {
 uint32_t IHS_SessionPacketTimestamp() {
     struct timespec tp;
     clock_gettime(CLOCK_MONOTONIC, &tp);
-    uint64_t nsec = tp.tv_nsec * 65536 / 1000000000;
-    uint32_t sec = tp.tv_sec * 65536;
-    return sec + nsec;
+    // Widen before multiplying: where long is 32 bits (WebAssembly), tv_nsec * 65536 overflows
+    // and the sub-second part came out as garbage. IHS_TimerNow had the same fix.
+    uint64_t nsec = (uint64_t) tp.tv_nsec * 65536 / 1000000000;
+    uint32_t sec = (uint32_t) ((uint64_t) tp.tv_sec * 65536);
+    return sec + (uint32_t) nsec;
 }
\ No newline at end of file
